consiSt("ne",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Nebraska Supreme Court","d":"Court of last resort. It hears direct appeals only in capital cases, life-imprisonment cases, and cases involving the constitutionality of a statute; other appeals go to the Court of Appeals unless the Supreme Court grants a petition to bypass or removes the case. It reviews Court of Appeals decisions by discretionary petition for further review. It writes the court rules (pleading, discovery, appellate practice) and controls bar admission, pro hac vice, and lawyer discipline (Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 24-1106, 24-1107; Neb. Ct. R. § 3-100 ff.)."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"Nebraska Court of Appeals","d":"Six judges, one appointed from each Supreme Court judicial district, sitting in rotating panels of three; a panel majority decides the case. A notice of appeal is treated as going to the Court of Appeals unless it expressly asks for the Supreme Court and cites the statute that sends the case there (Neb. Rev. Stat. § 24-1101; Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-101(B)(1)(a))."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"District Courts","d":"General original and appellate jurisdiction in civil and criminal matters, organized into 12 district court judicial districts (District 4, Douglas County, has 18 judges; District 3, Lancaster County, has 8). District courts also hear appeals from the county courts (Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 24-301.02, 24-302, 25-2728). Practice is governed by the Uniform District Court Rules, Neb. Ct. R. § 6-1501 ff., plus approved local rules."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"County Courts (including Small Claims Court)","d":"Exclusive original jurisdiction over decedents’ estates, guardianships, and conservatorships, and concurrent jurisdiction with the district court over civil actions up to an amount set by the Supreme Court (the current figure is not in the collected texts). If the amount in controversy exceeds the limit, any party may have the case certified to district court. Small Claims Court handles claims up to $7,500 (since July 1, 2025). The County Court Expedited Civil Actions Act (Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 25-2741 to 25-2749) offers a streamlined track with mandatory initial disclosures (Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 24-517(5), 25-2802(4); Neb. Ct. R. § 6-2201 ff.). Separate juvenile courts and the Workers’ Compensation Court also exist; their rules were not collected."}],"slapp":[{"t":"Statute: Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 25-21,241 to 25-21,246 (1994), narrow in scope","d":"Nebraska’s anti-SLAPP law covers only an “action involving public petition and participation”: a claim, cross-claim, or counterclaim for damages brought by a “public applicant or permittee” (someone who applied for or obtained a permit, zoning change, lease, license, certificate, or other permission from a government body, or someone affiliated with that person) that is materially related to the defendant’s efforts to report on, comment on, rule on, challenge, or oppose the application or permission. It does not reach speech on public issues generally. The statute dates from Laws 1994, LB 665, and the chapter 25 index on the Legislature’s site shows no newer anti-SLAPP or Uniform Public Expression Protection Act sections.","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 25-21,241(4), 25-21,242(1), (4)","v":true},{"t":"Procedure: an ordinary motion to dismiss or for summary judgment with a shifted burden; no deadline, no discovery stay","d":"A motion to dismiss for failure to state a cause of action must be granted once the movant shows the claim is an action involving public petition and participation, unless the responding party shows the claim has a substantial basis in law or is supported by a substantial argument to extend, modify, or reverse existing law. A summary-judgment motion works the same way, except the respondent must show a substantial basis in fact and law. The court must expedite the motion to dismiss and give both motions preference in hearing. The statute sets no filing deadline and does not stay discovery.","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 25-21,245, 25-21,246","v":true},{"t":"Heightened standard for the plaintiff’s damages","d":"In such an action, the plaintiff may recover damages only by proving by clear and convincing evidence that the communication was made knowing it was false or with reckless disregard of whether it was false, when truth or falsity is material to the claim.","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-21,244(1)","v":true},{"t":"Fees and damages require a separate claim, not a motion","d":"There is no automatic fee award on a successful motion. The defendant must bring its own action, claim, cross-claim, or counterclaim. Costs and attorney’s fees are recoverable on showing the suit was commenced or continued without a substantial basis in fact and law and could not be supported by a substantial argument for changing the law. Other compensatory damages also require proof that the suit was brought to harass, intimidate, punish, or maliciously inhibit petition, speech, or association. The right may be waived only by a specific waiver. The Revisor’s annotation notes that the trial court decides the first showing as a matter of law (Sand Livestock Sys. v. Svoboda, 17 Neb. App. 28 (2008)).","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-21,243 and annotation","v":true},{"t":"Appeal: no special right","d":"The statute gives no immediate appeal from denial of an anti-SLAPP motion. Denial of a motion to dismiss or for summary judgment is not among the listed final orders (the only listed summary-judgment denial is one based on sovereign or official immunity), so review would normally wait for final judgment. No case law on this point was researched.","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 25-21,245, 25-21,246, 25-1902(1)","v":false}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice: motion by Nebraska counsel, $250 per case per court, oath after admission","d":"A lawyer of good moral character admitted and practicing in another state, D.C., or a U.S. territory may be admitted in the court’s (or agency’s) discretion for a specific case. The Nebraska lawyer files the motion (Appendix B for courts) as soon as possible and no later than the date the applicant files any pleading or appears personally. A separate motion is needed in each case, but one motion covers cases consolidated by order.\nThe applicant’s application states the parties represented, every jurisdiction of licensure with bar numbers, good standing and any pending discipline or investigation (with details), and that the applicant is subject to the Nebraska Rules of Professional Conduct.\nFee: $250 to the clerk of each court or agency, per case; one fee for consolidated cases. It is refunded if the motion is denied and may be waived on written motion for government or indigent pro bono representation. A lawyer admitted in the Court of Appeals need not reapply or pay again if the same appeal moves to the Supreme Court.\nAfter the motion is granted, the lawyer must take and file the oath in Neb. Rev. Stat. § 7-104.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. § 3-122(A)-(F); Neb. Rev. Stat. § 7-104","v":true},{"t":"Local counsel must be a Nebraska resident who signs everything and attends everything","d":"Unless exempt under Neb. Rev. Stat. § 7-103, the applicant must associate with a Nebraska-resident lawyer admitted in Nebraska, who accepts service for the case. That lawyer (or a designee) must sign the pro hac vice motion and every pleading, motion, and paper filed in the case, and must personally appear at all proceedings unless the court excuses it.\nReciprocity exemption: under § 7-103, lawyers from states whose laws let Nebraska lawyers practice there without local counsel are not required to comply with that section. Check your home state’s rule before relying on this.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. § 3-122(E); Neb. Rev. Stat. § 7-103","v":true},{"t":"E-filing: pro hac vice counsel cannot file; Nebraska counsel files everything","d":"E-filing, e-service, and e-notice are mandatory for all Nebraska attorneys in all trial and appellate courts, and every Nebraska attorney who files or appears must register with the court-authorized service provider (annual fee set by the provider). For a lawyer admitted pro hac vice, all electronic filings must be made exclusively by the associated Nebraska counsel. Pro hac vice lawyers do receive notice from the clerk.\nA document received by 11:59:59 p.m. in the court’s local time zone is filed that day. Use the system-generated certificate of service, not a separate certificate.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. §§ 2-202(A), 2-203(A), (C)(1), 2-205(B), 2-206, 2-208(D)","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope work: “Prepared By” ghostwriting and limited appearances","d":"A Nebraska lawyer may draft pleadings, briefs, or other documents for a self-represented litigant, but the filing must show “Prepared By” with the lawyer’s name, business address, and bar number. The litigant signs as “pro se”; the lawyer does not sign, and this is not an appearance.\nWith the client’s written consent after consultation, a lawyer may enter a “Limited Appearance” that clearly defines the scope. Within 10 days after finishing, the lawyer must file a “Certificate of Completion of Limited Representation” and give copies to the client and the opposing side. Filing the certificate withdraws the appearance without court approval.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. of Prof. Cond. § 3-501.2(c)-(e); Neb. Ct. R. Pldg. § 6-1111(b)","v":true},{"t":"Temporary practice and in-house counsel","d":"Nebraska’s multijurisdictional-practice rule tracks ABA Model Rule 5.5(c): temporary services in association with an actively participating Nebraska lawyer, services related to a proceeding where the lawyer is or expects to be authorized, related ADR, or work arising out of the home-state practice. Services to an employer are allowed only if the lawyer is registered as in-house counsel under Neb. Ct. R. §§ 3-1201 to 3-1204 (not collected). Separately, filing papers in a Nebraska court record without a Nebraska admission is a Class III misdemeanor under the unauthorized-practice statute.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. of Prof. Cond. § 3-505.5(c)-(d); Neb. Rev. Stat. § 7-101","v":true},{"t":"Discovery for an out-of-state case: $75 clerk-issued subpoena, but motions need a Nebraska lawyer","d":"To take a deposition or get documents in Nebraska for a case pending elsewhere, submit to the clerk of the district court in the county where discovery will happen a request in the form of the rule’s appendix, with the foreign subpoena and a list of all counsel, and pay $75 per subpoena ($75 again for any reissue). Requesting the subpoena is not an appearance or unauthorized practice. A lawyer not admitted in Nebraska certifies admission in the foreign jurisdiction and no disbarment or suspension anywhere. But a motion to quash, enforce, modify, or for a protective order must be filed as a civil action in that district court, and only a Nebraska-admitted attorney or a self-represented individual may file or oppose it.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. Disc. § 6-330(A)(b), (e), (f)","v":true},{"t":"Admission without examination","d":"Admission on motion is available to (A) UBE or NextGen UBE takers with Nebraska’s minimum score plus the MPRE, (B) non-UBE takers with an equivalent score, and (C) lawyers who passed any bar exam where licensed and actively practiced for 3 of the last 5 years (or were registered Nebraska in-house counsel). Under (A) and (B), bar exam and MPRE scores more than 5 years old are not accepted. A reduced-fee track exists for military spouses. All motion applicants must submit fingerprints.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. § 3-119(A)-(D), (F)","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: Nebraska Rules of Professional Conduct, Neb. Ct. R. of Prof. Cond. §§ 3-501.0 to 3-508.5","d":"Nebraska’s rules follow the ABA Model Rules numbering, with a “3-50” prefix (Model Rule 1.6 is § 3-501.6). The text internally cites rules by ABA number (for example, “Rule 1.7”). A pro hac vice applicant must affirm being subject to these rules.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. of Prof. Cond. § 3-501.0 ff.; Neb. Ct. R. § 3-122(D)","v":true},{"t":"Confidentiality: broader “any crime” exception, but no ABA financial-fraud or conflict-check exceptions","d":"A lawyer may reveal information to prevent the client from committing a crime (any crime, not limited to crimes causing financial injury) or to prevent reasonably certain death or substantial bodily harm. The Nebraska rule has no counterparts to ABA Model Rule 1.6(b)(2)-(3) (fraud using the lawyer’s services, including rectifying loss) or 1.6(b)(7) (conflict checks when changing firms), and no ABA 1.6(c) duty to make reasonable efforts to prevent unauthorized disclosure. Nebraska’s § 3-501.6(c) instead makes the relationship with the Nebraska Lawyers Assistance Program confidential like a client relationship.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. of Prof. Cond. § 3-501.6(b)-(c)","v":true},{"t":"No screening for lateral hires","d":"Conflicts are imputed across the firm except for personal-interest conflicts that pose no significant risk. Nebraska’s § 3-501.10 has no counterpart to ABA Model Rule 1.10(a)(2), which lets a firm screen a laterally hired lawyer to avoid imputing the lawyer’s former-client conflicts. Without client consent, a lateral’s conflict can disqualify the whole firm.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. of Prof. Cond. § 3-501.10(a), (c)","v":true},{"t":"Fees: free itemized accounting on request; no contingent fees in divorce or criminal defense","d":"On a reasonable and timely request, a lawyer must give the client a free accounting of fees and costs, itemizing hourly charges, costs, interest, and past-due balances, and describing hourly services and who performed them. This duty has no ABA counterpart. A contingent-fee agreement must be in writing signed by the client and must clearly tell the client about any expenses owed whether or not the client prevails. Contingent fees in domestic-relations and criminal-defense matters are barred, as in the ABA rule.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. of Prof. Cond. § 3-501.5(c), (d), (f)","v":true},{"t":"Misconduct: anti-discrimination in § 3-508.4(d) and refusal to pay support","d":"Nebraska has not adopted ABA Model Rule 8.4(g). Instead, § 3-508.4(d) says a lawyer acting professionally must not engage in adverse discriminatory treatment of litigants, witnesses, lawyers, judges, judicial officers, or court personnel based on race, national origin, gender, religion, disability, age, sexual orientation, or socio-economic status. Legitimate advocacy is excepted. Under § 3-508.4(g), willfully refusing (as determined by a court) to timely pay a support order is misconduct.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. of Prof. Cond. § 3-508.4(d), (g)","v":true},{"t":"Solicitation: “This is an advertisement” labels still required","d":"Every written, recorded, or electronic communication soliciting employment from a prospective client must say “This is an advertisement” on the outside envelope, at the beginning and end of any recorded or electronic communication, and in an email subject line, unless the recipient is a lawyer or has a family, close personal, or prior professional relationship with the lawyer. ABA Model Rule 7.3 no longer requires this label.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. of Prof. Cond. § 3-507.3(c)","v":true}],"style":"State of Nebraska","home":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"Serve every defendant within 180 days or the case dismisses itself","d":"An action is commenced when the complaint is filed. Each defendant must be properly served within 180 days, or the action against that defendant is dismissed without prejudice by operation of law the day after the time expires. The Revisor’s annotations describe the statute as self-executing; courts cannot extend it.","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-217 and annotations"},{"t":"A notice of appeal filed before a post-trial motion is decided is void","d":"A timely motion for new trial, to alter or amend, or to set aside the verdict (each due 10 days after entry of judgment) terminates the 30-day appeal time for all parties. A notice of appeal filed before the court announces its decision on such a motion has no effect, whether it was filed before or after the motion. You must file a new notice within 30 days after entry of the order ruling on the motion (no new fee). Unlike FRAP 4(a)(4)(B)(i), the early notice does not ripen.","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 25-1912(3), 25-1144.01, 25-1329, 25-1315.02"},{"t":"Order the bill of exceptions with the notice of appeal, or lose it","d":"The appellant must file the request for the bill of exceptions (the transcript of the evidence) in the trial court at the same time as the notice of appeal. A late request is a waiver unless the appellate court grants leave for good cause outside the party’s reasonable control. Separately, file the transcript request designating each pleading by name and filing date; generalized “all documents” requests are not allowed.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. §§ 2-105(B)(2)(a), (f), 2-104(A)(1)"},{"t":"Errors not separately assigned are not reviewed","d":"The appellant’s brief must include a separate, concise statement of each claimed error, separately numbered and paragraphed. Review is limited to errors both assigned and discussed in the brief; plain error is reviewed only at the court’s option. Cross-appeals go in a separate section of the appellee’s brief titled “Brief on Cross-Appeal.”","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-109(D)(1)(e), (D)(4); Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-1919"},{"t":"Excessive or inadequate damages need a new-trial motion","d":"In a jury case, a motion for new trial is not required to challenge the sufficiency of the evidence, but it is a prerequisite to appellate review of inadequate or excessive damages. The motion is due 10 days after entry of judgment.","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 25-1912.01(2), 25-1144.01"},{"t":"Do not plead a dollar amount for general damages","d":"A complaint must not state the amount of general damages. It must state each category and the total of special damages, and if you want prejudgment interest, the date from which it runs.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. Pldg. § 6-1108(a)(3)(A)-(C)"},{"t":"Improper venue is a motion to transfer, not a Rule 12(b)(3) dismissal","d":"Section 6-1112(b)(3) is “pendency of another action,” not venue. A venue objection is raised by a timely motion to transfer to a proper county. The transferee court may order the plaintiff or its lawyer to pay the defendant’s expenses, including attorney’s fees.","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-403.01; Neb. Ct. R. Pldg. § 6-1112(b)(3) & cmt. [7]"},{"t":"Answer in 30 days, and only one pre-answer motion","d":"The answer is due 30 days after service, not 21. A party that files a § 6-1112 motion cannot file a second pre-answer motion raising a defense that was available but omitted. That applies even to failure to state a claim on a new ground, which must instead be raised in the answer, by motion for judgment on the pleadings, or at trial.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. Pldg. § 6-1112(a)(1)(A), (g)(2), (h)(2) & cmts. [11]-[12]"},{"t":"Summary judgment: evidence is offered at a hearing, with indexes and annotated statements","d":"Serve the motion at least 10 days before the hearing; the opponent may serve affidavits before the hearing day. In district court, the movant must file and serve an Evidence Index and an Annotated Statement of Undisputed Facts with pinpoint cites. The opponent files an Evidence Index and an Annotated Statement of Disputed Facts. Leaving out the statement can be grounds to deny (or grant) the motion. Do not put these documents in the brief, and do not file discovery materials; offer them as evidence at the hearing.","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-1332(1); Neb. Ct. R. § 6-1526; Neb. Ct. R. Pldg. § 6-1105 cmt. [9]"},{"t":"Nonparty document subpoenas need 14 days’ notice to the parties first","d":"Before issuing a subpoena for documents or entry to a nonparty, serve every party with written notice at least 14 days before issuance. Any party may object, and then the subpoena may not command the objected-to items until the objection is withdrawn or overruled. Serve the subpoena at least 14 days before the production date, with copies to the other parties. The nonparty may object in writing within 14 days.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. Disc. § 6-334(A)(b)-(e)"},{"t":"Expert disclosures are due by default 180 days after the first answer","d":"Since 2025, Nebraska requires federal-style expert disclosures, with signed reports from retained experts. Absent a court order or stipulation, they are due within 180 days after the first responsive pleading is served, and rebuttal disclosures within 45 days after the other side’s disclosure. A treating physician need not write a report merely because the testimony covers causation or prognosis.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. Disc. § 6-326(c)(1)-(2)"},{"t":"Supersedeas and cost bond within 30 days","d":"An appeal does not stay a money judgment unless, within 30 days after entry, the appellant posts a supersedeas bond, U.S. Government bonds, or cash. The amount is the lesser of the judgment plus costs and estimated interest, 50% of the appellant’s net worth, or $50 million. If there is no supersedeas bond, a $75 cost bond or cash deposit is due within 30 days. If it is not given, the appeal may be dismissed on motion.","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 25-1916(1), 25-1914; Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-101(G)(2)"}],"judge":[{"t":"No peremptory change of judge; disqualification for cause only","d":"None of the collected Nebraska statutes or rules provides a peremptory challenge or automatic substitution of a judge. By statute, a judge at any level is disqualified (unless all parties consent in writing on the record) when the judge is a party or interested; is related to a party within the fourth degree; is related to an attorney in a pending county or district court case as parent, child, sibling, or in-law (or is that attorney’s partner); was counsel for a party; or shares office space with a former law partner who is retained or interested in the case.","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. § 24-739","v":true},{"t":"Code of Judicial Conduct: the ABA impartiality standard, with remittal","d":"A judge must step aside whenever impartiality might reasonably be questioned, including for personal bias, personal knowledge of disputed facts, family relationships with a party or lawyer, economic interest, prior work as lawyer, mediator, or government lawyer in the matter, being a material witness, or having previously presided over the matter in another court. Nebraska’s list leaves out the ABA campaign-contribution ground (paragraph (A)(4) is “Reserved”). Except for bias, the parties and lawyers may agree, outside the judge’s presence, to waive the disqualification; the agreement goes into the record. The collected rules do not set a motion procedure or deadline for seeking recusal.","c":"Neb. Rev. Code of Judicial Conduct § 5-302.11(A), (C)","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"Statutory actual-malice standard in permit-related suits","d":"When a public applicant or permittee sues over opposition to its permit, zoning change, license, or similar application, it may recover damages only by proving by clear and convincing evidence that the communication was knowingly false or made with reckless disregard for the truth. This is a statutory actual-malice requirement that applies whether or not the plaintiff is a public figure.","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-21,244","v":true},{"t":"State constitutional speech protection: not checked","d":"The Nebraska Constitution was not collected, and neither was case law on whether its speech guarantee is read more broadly than the First Amendment. Nothing is claimed here.","c":"Neb. Const. (not in collected texts)","v":false}],"other":[{"t":"Civil procedure is a mix of statute and court rule","d":"Nebraska has no single code of civil procedure modeled on the Federal Rules. The Legislature directed the Supreme Court to adopt pleading rules for actions filed on or after January 1, 2003, and abolished demurrers, special appearances, pleas in bar or abatement, and cross-petitions. Commencement, service of summons, third-party practice, joinder, venue, summary judgment, new trial, judgments, and appeals are all statutory (Neb. Rev. Stat. ch. 25). The pleading and discovery rules leave reserved sections where the statutes govern, and they yield to any inconsistent statute. Both rule sets were extensively amended effective January 1, 2025 (again September 3, 2025); for cases pending on January 1, 2025, the court may order that the old version applies.","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-801.01; Neb. Ct. R. Pldg. §§ 6-1101(a), (c), 6-1102 to 6-1104, 6-1114; Neb. Ct. R. Disc. § 6-326(h)","v":true},{"t":"Mandatory fee-shifting for frivolous claims and defenses","d":"Instead of a Rule 11 sanctions regime, the court must award reasonable attorney’s fees and costs against any attorney or party who brought or defended a claim the court finds frivolous or made in bad faith, or interposed solely for delay or harassment, or who unnecessarily expanded the proceedings (including discovery abuse). There is no fee award for a good-faith attempt to establish a new theory of law, or if the claim is voluntarily dismissed within a reasonable time after the party knew or should have known it would not prevail. The current text, last amended by Laws 2026, LB935 (operative July 18, 2026), also gives political subdivisions a separate-hearing procedure to seek fees against frivolous or harassing claims or defenses.","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-824(2)-(6); Neb. Ct. R. Pldg. § 6-1111 cmt. [1]","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Nebraska Court Rules of Appellate Practice, Neb. Ct. R. App. P. §§ 2-101 to 2-118 (official text on nebraskajudicial.gov; latest amendment shown § 2-106(E), August 20, 2025). Electronic Filing, Service, and Notice System rules, Neb. Ct. R. §§ 2-201 to 2-217. Uniform District Court Rules § 6-1518 (county-court appeals). Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 24-1101, 24-1104, 24-1106, 24-1107, 25-1144.01, 25-1315, 25-1315.02, 25-1329, 25-1902, 25-1911 to 25-1916, 25-1919, 25-2728, 25-2729, from nebraskalegislature.gov, fetched November 10, 2026.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in Nebraska: a statutory 30-day clock, a void-if-early notice, and a record you must order up front","d":"Civil appeals from the district court are governed mainly by statute (Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-1912) and the Supreme Court’s appellate rules. The appeal is perfected, and the appellate court gets jurisdiction, when the notice of appeal is filed and the docket fee deposited (or in forma pauperis status granted) in the trial court within 30 days after entry of the judgment or final order. Entry is the clerk’s file stamp date. Only filing the notice and depositing the fee are jurisdictional.\nAppeals go to the six-judge Court of Appeals by default. The Supreme Court hears a case directly only in the categories set by statute, on a petition to bypass, or by removal. It reviews the Court of Appeals by discretionary petition for further review.\nThe main traps: a notice of appeal filed while a post-trial motion is pending is void, the request for the bill of exceptions must be filed together with the notice, and the court reviews only errors that are separately assigned and argued.","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 25-1301(3), 25-1912(1), (3)-(4), 24-1106; Neb. Ct. R. App. P. §§ 2-101(A), 2-105(B)(2)(a), 2-109(D)(1)(e)"},"groups":[["Which court, and what can be appealed",[{"t":"Court of Appeals is the default","d":"A notice of appeal is deemed made to the Court of Appeals unless it specifically asks for the Supreme Court and cites the statute directing the appeal there. By statute, appeals go to the Court of Appeals except capital cases, life-imprisonment cases, and cases involving the constitutionality of a statute. The Court of Appeals sits in rotating three-judge panels.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-101(B)(1)(a); Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 24-1106(1), 24-1101(1)"},{"t":"Final orders","d":"Appealable final orders are (a) an order affecting a substantial right that in effect determines the action and prevents a judgment, (b) an order affecting a substantial right made in a special proceeding, (c) an order affecting a substantial right made on summary application after judgment, and (d) denial of summary judgment based on sovereign immunity or a government official’s immunity. A type (d) order may be appealed within 30 days after its entry or within 30 days after entry of judgment.","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-1902(1)-(2)"},{"t":"Partial judgments need express findings","d":"When there are multiple claims or parties, an order resolving fewer than all is final only if the court expressly determines there is no just reason for delay and expressly directs entry of judgment. Otherwise it can be revised at any time before final judgment. The appellant’s jurisdictional statement must quote that language (or cite other authority for the interlocutory appeal).","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-1315(1); Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-109(D)(1)(c)(iv)"},{"t":"No general permissive interlocutory appeal found","d":"The collected statutes and rules contain no procedure like 28 U.S.C. § 1292(b) for discretionary review of interlocutory orders. Outside the final-order categories and § 25-1315 certification, review normally waits for final judgment. Special statutes not collected (for example, for particular proceedings) may provide otherwise.","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 25-1902, 25-1315"},{"t":"County court cases go first to district court","d":"Appeals from a final judgment or order of the county court go to the district court of the same county. To perfect the appeal, file a notice of appeal with the county court clerk and deposit the district court docket fee within 30 days after entry, and serve the notice on all parties who appeared. Small-claims appeals also need a $50 cash bond or surety undertaking. In the district court, the appellant must file a statement of errors within 20 days after the bill of exceptions is filed, or within 30 days after the notice of appeal if no bill was requested. Review is limited to errors assigned, plus plain error. Probate, juvenile, adoption, domestic-relations, and some other county-court matters are excluded from this route.","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 25-2728, 25-2729(1), (4), (7); Neb. Ct. R. § 6-1518(B)"}]],["Starting the appeal",[{"t":"Notice of appeal and docket fee: 30 days, in the trial court","d":"File in the office of the district court clerk, within 30 days after entry of the judgment, decree, or final order, a notice of intention to prosecute the appeal signed by the appellant or its attorney of record, and deposit the docket fee (Neb. Rev. Stat. § 33-103; amount not collected). A notice or fee filed after the decision is announced but before entry is treated as filed on the date of entry. The trial court clerk forwards the notice, the transcript and bill-of-exceptions requests, and a certificate to the Clerk of the Supreme Court within 2 business days.","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 25-1912(1)-(2), (4)-(5), 25-1301(3); Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-101(A)-(B)"},{"t":"Transcript request at the same time","d":"With the notice of appeal, file a request directing the clerk to prepare the transcript (the court file). Designate each pleading or document by name and filing date; generalized requests for all documents, or a printed register of actions, are not allowed. The transcript always includes the judgment or order appealed from, the operative pleadings, and the bond record. Do not request exhibits. The clerk transmits the transcript electronically within 10 days. A supplemental transcript may be requested without leave before submission.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-104(A)-(C); Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-1912(6)"},{"t":"Bill of exceptions request with the notice, or waived","d":"The request for the bill of exceptions (verbatim record and exhibits) must be filed with the trial court clerk at the same time as the notice of appeal and served on the appellee. It must identify each portion of the evidence and exhibits believed material. If you will argue that a finding is unsupported by the evidence, it must include all evidence relevant to that finding. A later request is a waiver unless the appellate court grants leave for good cause outside the party’s reasonable control. The appellee may file a supplemental request within 10 days after service of the appellant’s request, or, if the appellant filed none, its own request within 10 days after the notice of appeal.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-105(B)(2)(a)-(f)"},{"t":"Cost deposit and timing of the bill","d":"Court reporting personnel must give the requesting party a cost estimate within 7 days. If the deposit is not timely made, the appeal proceeds as if no bill was requested unless the appellate court extends the time for good cause. In civil cases, the bill is due 7 weeks after the date the deposit was due, subject to extensions approved by the appellate court.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. §§ 2-105(B)(4)(b)-(c), 2-105.01(B)"},{"t":"Cost bond and supersedeas","d":"Within 30 days after entry of the judgment (or of the order overruling a new-trial motion), file a $75 cost bond or make a $75 cash deposit, unless a supersedeas bond is given. The appeal may be dismissed on motion if this is not done. An appeal stays a money judgment only if, within 30 days after entry, the appellant posts a supersedeas bond, U.S. Government bonds, or cash in the lesser of (a) the judgment plus costs and estimated interest and appeal costs, (b) 50% of the appellant’s net worth, or (c) $50 million. The cap can be lifted if the appellant is shown to be dissipating assets. Other caps and conditions apply to judgments for conveyances, real estate, and injunctions.","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 25-1914, 25-1916; Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-101(G)(2)"},{"t":"Counsel and e-filing","d":"Trial counsel of record remain counsel on appeal until the appellate court grants withdrawal. Filings in both appellate courts are electronic through the court-authorized service provider, as text-searchable PDFs. Paper filing is limited to self-represented parties who are not registered users. Pro hac vice counsel’s filings must be made by associated Nebraska counsel.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. §§ 2-101(F)(2), (H), 2-103(A)(1); Neb. Ct. R. § 2-203(C)(1)"}]],["Post-trial motions and tolling",[{"t":"Three tolling motions, each due 10 days after entry of judgment","d":"A motion for new trial, a motion to alter or amend the judgment, and a motion to set aside the verdict or judgment after a denied directed-verdict motion are each due no later than 10 days after entry of judgment. One filed after the verdict or decision is announced but before entry counts as filed on the day of entry.","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 25-1144.01, 25-1329, 25-1315.02"},{"t":"Tolling resets the full 30 days, and an early notice is void","d":"A timely terminating motion by any party stops the appeal clock for all parties, and the full 30 days runs from entry of the order ruling on it. A notice of appeal filed before the court announces its decision on the motion has no effect, whether filed before or after the motion. A new notice must be filed (no additional fee).","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-1912(3)"},{"t":"When a new-trial motion is required","d":"A new-trial motion is not a prerequisite to appellate review of any issue on which the trial court’s ruling appears in the record, including sufficiency of the evidence after a jury trial. It is required to challenge inadequate or excessive damages in a jury case.","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-1912.01"}]],["Cross-appeals",[{"t":"No separate notice: assert it in the appellee’s brief","d":"A properly filed appeal gives every appellee the right to cross-appeal against any other party. The cross-appeal is asserted only in the appellee’s brief, noted on the cover, in a separate section headed “Brief on Cross-Appeal” that follows the rules for an appellant’s brief. The appellant answers in a separate “Answer to Brief on Cross-Appeal” in the reply brief. All later attempts by other parties to appeal are filed in the same docketed case.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. §§ 2-101(C), (E), 2-109(D)(4)-(5)"},{"t":"Keep the cross-appeal alive if the appellant dismisses","d":"If the appellant moves to dismiss its appeal, a party with a right to cross-appeal may file a notice of intention to cross-appeal within 10 days after the motion is filed. The court then denies the motion and sets a brief date, and the case proceeds as if the cross-appellant had perfected the appeal.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-108(E)"},{"t":"No cross-appeals at later stages","d":"The right of cross-appeal does not apply to petitions for further review, supplemental briefing on further review, or motions for rehearing. A party must file its own petition or motion instead.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. §§ 2-101(E), 2-102(F)(4), (H), 2-113(F)"}]],["Briefs: timing, form, and limits",[{"t":"Briefing schedule","d":"Appellant’s brief: 30 days after the transcript is filed in the appellate court if no bill of exceptions was requested, or 30 days after the bill of exceptions is due if one was. Appellee’s brief: 30 days after the appellant serves and files. Reply: 10 days after the appellee serves and files. Add 3 days if the brief was served by mail. Filing a motion for summary dismissal or affirmance does not extend brief dates.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. §§ 2-109(A)(1)-(3), 2-107(B)(8)"},{"t":"Extensions are tiered","d":"A first extension (except in juvenile cases) needs only a general showing of need and may be granted immediately, for up to 30 days. A second request, or a first request in a juvenile case, needs good cause, such as unavailable records, complex issues, specific conflicts with another court, or new counsel. “Press of other business” and stipulations are not good cause. Third and later requests need exceptional cause, such as matters beyond the requester’s control or unexpected illness. In advanced cases, no extension is allowed except for exceptional cause.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. §§ 2-106(E), 2-109(A)"},{"t":"Word limits are cumulative per party","d":"15,000 words total per party on original submission. For the appellant, that covers the opening brief, reply, and answer to any cross-appeal combined; for the appellee, its brief, the cross-appeal brief, and any cross-reply combined. The limit rises to 18,000 words per party if the appellee cross-appeals. Everything counts, including the cover, tables, and signature blocks. Briefs supporting rehearing motions or other motions, and amicus briefs, are limited to 3,800 words. The last page must certify the word count, the software name and version, and typeface compliance.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-103(C)(3)-(4)"},{"t":"Format","d":"8½ × 11 portrait, text-searchable PDF, left-aligned (not justified), 1.5-inch margins, 1.15 or 1.2 line spacing, no footnotes. Century or Century Schoolbook preferred (Times New Roman, Baskerville Old Face, Book Antiqua, or Palatino allowed) at 12-13 point, with no underlining. Hyperlinks only to the record, internal bookmarks, the official Nebraska Reports, the Legislature’s site, and the court rules. The cover is the title page and must list the case number, caption (plaintiff first), county, trial judge, and attorney information including the Nebraska bar number.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-103(A), (C)(1)-(2)"},{"t":"Required contents","d":"The appellant’s brief contains, in order: cover; tables; a jurisdictional statement (dates of judgment, any tolling motion and its disposition, notice of appeal and fee, and the basis for any partial-judgment appeal); a statement of the case; separately numbered assignments of error; propositions of law, each followed by authorities; a statement of facts with every fact cited to the record (T26, 156:12, E5, p. 95); a summary of argument; and the argument. Only errors assigned and discussed are considered, though the court may notice plain error.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-109(C)(1)-(3), (D)(1); Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-1919"},{"t":"Challenging a statute’s constitutionality","d":"A party claiming a Nebraska statute is unconstitutional must file a separate notice with the Clerk after docketing and before filing its brief. If the Attorney General is not already in the case, the brief must be served on the Attorney General. Without the notice, the Supreme Court will not consider the constitutional question except by special order.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-109(E)"},{"t":"Briefs are public; amicus needs leave","d":"Briefs are not confidential and may not reproduce confidential material; cite to it instead. An amicus must move for leave at least 30 days before oral argument and file any permitted brief at least 10 days before argument. Leave may be denied if it would cause a recusal.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-109(A)(4), (D)"}]],["Motions, submission, and argument",[{"t":"Motion practice","d":"Responses to motions are due within 10 days of filing, and there are no replies. Motions are submitted on stipulation, when all responses are in, or when the 10-day period ends. There is no oral argument on motions unless ordered (5 minutes per side). A motion to dismiss for lack of jurisdiction may be filed any time after docketing. A motion to affirm as too unsubstantial to need argument may be filed after the appellant’s brief is filed or due.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. §§ 2-106(B), (D), 2-107(B)"},{"t":"Summary disposition on the court’s own motion","d":"The court may dismiss for lack of jurisdiction, or summarily affirm when one of the listed grounds is dispositive and a detailed opinion would have no precedential value. It may summarily reverse only after an order to show cause. Parties cannot request summary affirmance under § 2-107(A).","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-107(A)"},{"t":"Scheduling and oral argument","d":"Cases may be submitted without argument; a party may move for argument within 7 days after the minute entry. Listed categories are advanced automatically (for example, workers’ compensation, child custody, juvenile, and certified questions). Other cases are scheduled in the order appellee briefs are filed. Argument is limited to 10 minutes per side unless ordered otherwise; a motion for more time is due within 10 days after the proposed call. Continuances require exceptional cause. A party without a brief on file may not argue.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-111(B)-(E)"},{"t":"Attorney fees on appeal: motion within 10 days","d":"A party claiming appellate attorney fees in a civil case must file a motion, citing the law or uniform course of practice that allows fees and supported by an affidavit, within 10 days after release of the opinion or the order disposing of the appeal (including an order on rehearing), unless a statute provides otherwise. A motion that cites no authority is summarily overruled. A timely fee motion in the Court of Appeals tolls the time to petition for further review.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-106(G)"},{"t":"Citing Court of Appeals opinions","d":"Only opinions designated “For Permanent Publication” may be cited freely, and they bind lower courts until the Supreme Court acts. Other Court of Appeals opinions may be cited only if persuasive on a material issue and no published opinion would serve as well, or if the case is related. Their unpublished status must be noted.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-102(E)(4)-(5)"}]],["Rehearing and review in the Supreme Court",[{"t":"Motion for rehearing: 10 days, brief required","d":"Within 10 days after release of the opinion or the order disposing of the appeal, file both the motion and a supporting brief (up to 3,800 words) containing tables, numbered assignments of error, propositions of law, and argument. Others may respond within 10 days. There is no oral argument. A timely rehearing motion in the Court of Appeals tolls the time to petition for further review. No rehearing is allowed after the Supreme Court denies further review. Failing to file the supporting brief on time can result in all costs being assessed against the movant.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. §§ 2-113(A)-(J), 2-103(C)(3)(b)"},{"t":"Petition for further review: 30 days, $50, 3,500 words","d":"File the petition and a memorandum brief within 30 days after the Court of Appeals’ opinion or its order finally disposing of the appeal, whichever is later. An order on rehearing or on attorney fees counts as a final disposition. The docket fee is $50 per case number and per petitioning party (waived for in forma pauperis). The combined length is limited to 3,500 words, with each Court of Appeals error separately assigned and annotated to the record. Responses are due within 10 days. There is no oral argument on the petition. Review is discretionary and limited to the errors assigned and discussed, though the Supreme Court may notice plain error.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-102(F)-(G); Neb. Rev. Stat. § 24-1107"},{"t":"If further review is granted","d":"The petitioner may file a supplemental brief within 20 days after the order granting review; other parties may file within 20 days after that. Supplemental briefs may not assert cross-appeals. The first petitioner opens and closes any argument.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-102(H)"},{"t":"Petition to bypass the Court of Appeals","d":"Any party may petition the Supreme Court to take the case directly. The petition is filed together with that party’s initial brief, limited to 1,800 words, and based on the § 24-1106(2) factors: first impression, constitutional interpretation, statutory validity, conflicting decisions, significant public interest, or qualified immunity in a § 1983 case. Responses are due with the responding party’s brief. Petitions are submitted when the reply brief is filed or due. The Supreme Court may also remove a case on its own motion or the Court of Appeals’ recommendation.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-102(B)-(C); Neb. Rev. Stat. § 24-1106(2)-(3)"},{"t":"Mandate and further review in the U.S. Supreme Court","d":"No mandate issues while a rehearing motion or petition for further review may be filed or is pending. To stay the mandate while seeking U.S. Supreme Court review, apply within 7 days after the opinion (with the rehearing motion, if one is filed), showing that a federal question is involved. The court may require a bond.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. §§ 2-114(A)(1)-(2), 2-102(F)(7)"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal and docket fee (district court civil judgment)","d":"30 days from entry of the judgment, decree, or final order (clerk’s file stamp date).\nFiled in the district court. The notice and fee are the only jurisdictional steps. No extension provision appears in § 25-1912.","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 25-1912(1), 25-1301(3)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the judgment, decree, or final order)","note":"Filed in the district court. The notice and fee are the only jurisdictional steps. No extension provision appears in § 25-1912."}},{"t":"Request for bill of exceptions; transcript request","d":"0 days from same time as the notice of appeal.\nA late bill-of-exceptions request is a waiver absent appellate leave for good cause outside the party’s control.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. §§ 2-105(B)(2)(a), 2-104(A)(1)","due":{"n":0,"from":"the trigger date (same time as the notice of appeal)","note":"A late bill-of-exceptions request is a waiver absent appellate leave for good cause outside the party’s control."}},{"t":"Appellee’s supplemental (or substitute) bill of exceptions request","d":"10 days from service of the appellant’s request (or the notice of appeal, if the appellant requested none).","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-105(B)(2)(e)-(f)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (service of the appellant’s request)","note":""}},{"t":"Motion for new trial / to alter or amend / to set aside verdict","d":"10 days from entry of judgment.\nA timely motion terminates the appeal time for all parties. An earlier notice of appeal is void; refile within 30 days after entry of the ruling.","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 25-1144.01, 25-1329, 25-1315.02","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"A timely motion terminates the appeal time for all parties. An earlier notice of appeal is void; refile within 30 days after entry of the ruling."}},{"t":"Notice of appeal after a tolling motion","d":"30 days from entry of the order ruling on the motion.\nNo additional fee.","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-1912(3)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order ruling on the motion)","note":"No additional fee."}},{"t":"Cost bond or $75 cash deposit","d":"30 days from entry of judgment or of the order overruling a new-trial motion.\nNot needed if a supersedeas bond is given. The appeal may be dismissed on motion if missing.","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-1914","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment or of the order overruling a new-trial motion)","note":"Not needed if a supersedeas bond is given. The appeal may be dismissed on motion if missing."}},{"t":"Supersedeas bond to stay execution","d":"30 days from entry of the judgment, decree, or final order.\nLesser of judgment-based amount, 50% of net worth, or $50 million for money judgments.","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-1916","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the judgment, decree, or final order)","note":"Lesser of judgment-based amount, 50% of net worth, or $50 million for money judgments."}},{"t":"Appeal from denial of summary judgment based on sovereign or official immunity","d":"30 days from entry of the order (or entry of judgment).","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-1902(1)(d), (2)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order)","note":""}},{"t":"Appellant’s brief","d":"30 days from filing of the transcript in the appellate court (no bill requested) or the bill of exceptions due date.\nFirst extension up to 30 days on a general showing; later ones need good or exceptional cause.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-109(A)(1)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the transcript in the appellate court)","note":"First extension up to 30 days on a general showing; later ones need good or exceptional cause."}},{"t":"Appellee’s brief (including any cross-appeal)","d":"30 days from appellant’s service and filing of its brief.\nAdd 3 days if served by mail.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. §§ 2-109(A)(2), 2-101(E)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (appellant’s service and filing of its brief)","note":"Add 3 days if served by mail."}},{"t":"Reply brief","d":"10 days from appellee’s service and filing of its brief.\nAdd 3 days if served by mail.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-109(A)(3)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (appellee’s service and filing of its brief)","note":"Add 3 days if served by mail."}},{"t":"Response to a motion","d":"10 days from filing of the motion.\nNo reply allowed.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-106(B)(2)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the motion)","note":"No reply allowed."}},{"t":"Motion for appellate attorney fees","d":"10 days from release of the opinion or order disposing of the appeal.\nUnless a statute provides otherwise.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-106(G)(2)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (release of the opinion or order disposing of the appeal)","note":"Unless a statute provides otherwise."}},{"t":"Motion for rehearing and supporting brief","d":"10 days from release of the opinion or order disposing of the appeal.\nTolls the time to petition for further review from the Court of Appeals.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-113(A)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (release of the opinion or order disposing of the appeal)","note":"Tolls the time to petition for further review from the Court of Appeals."}},{"t":"Petition for further review and memorandum brief","d":"30 days from release of the Court of Appeals opinion or entry of its final order, whichever is later.\n$50 docket fee; 3,500 words; responses due within 10 days.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-102(F)(1); Neb. Rev. Stat. § 24-1107","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (release of the Court of Appeals opinion or entry of its final order, whichever is later)","note":"$50 docket fee; 3,500 words; responses due within 10 days."}},{"t":"Supplemental brief after further review is granted","d":"20 days from entry of the order granting further review (nonpetitioners: 20 days after the petitioner’s brief).","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-102(H)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order granting further review)","note":""}},{"t":"Application to stay mandate pending U.S. Supreme Court review","d":"7 days from filing of the opinion or other dispositive entry.\nMust show that a federal question is involved.","c":"Neb. Ct. R. App. P. § 2-114(A)(2)","due":{"n":7,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the opinion or other dispositive entry)","note":"Must show that a federal question is involved."}},{"t":"Appeal from county court to district court","d":"30 days from entry of the county court judgment or final order.\nNotice and district court docket fee are filed with the county court clerk. The statement of errors is due 20 days after the bill of exceptions is filed (30 days after the notice if no bill) (§ 6-1518(B)).","c":"Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-2729(1)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the county court judgment or final order)","note":"Notice and district court docket fee are filed with the county court clerk. The statement of errors is due 20 days after the bill of exceptions is filed (30 days after the notice if no bill) (§ 6-1518(B))."}}]]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/"]]},"rulesName":"Nebraska Court Rules of Pleading in Civil Cases","rulesSrc":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"6-1101","t":"Scope and purpose","s":"The pleading rules govern civil actions filed on or after January 1, 2003, but only to the extent they are consistent with applicable statutes; a statute with different or additional pleading requirements controls. They are to be construed and used by the court and parties to secure a just and speedy determination without undue cost (Nebraska’s variation on FRCP 1’s “inexpensive”). The 2025 amendments apply to cases filed on or after January 1, 2025, and to pending cases, unless the court orders the prior version for a pending case as impracticable, unreasonable, or unjust.","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"},{"n":"6-1102","t":"One form of action [Reserved]","s":"No text. The single civil action is created by statute (Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-101), not by rule.","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"},{"n":"6-1103","t":"Commencement of action [Reserved]","s":"No text. Commencement is statutory: an action is commenced when the complaint is filed, and each defendant must be served within 180 days or the action against that defendant is dismissed by operation of law (Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-217).","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"},{"n":"6-1104","t":"Summons [Reserved]","s":"No text. Service of process is governed by Neb. Rev. Stat. ch. 25, art. 5 (for example, personal, residence, certified mail, or designated delivery service under § 25-505.01), not by an FRCP 4 counterpart.","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"},{"n":"6-1105","t":"Serving and filing pleadings and other documents","s":"Later pleadings, written motions, required discovery documents, and notices, appearances, demands, and offers of judgment must be served on every party. Unlike FRCP 5, a party in default for not appearing must still be served with a motion for default judgment and the notice of hearing on it, and with any pleading asserting a new claim (served like a summons). Attorneys must e-file and e-serve through the court-authorized service provider (§§ 2-203, 2-205). Other documents may be served by hand, by leaving them at the office or residence, by mail (complete on mailing), by email to a stated address, by designated delivery service, or by consented or court-authorized means. Discovery documents and discovery subpoenas are not filed unless relevant to a motion or ordered; documents not served electronically need a certificate of service within a reasonable time.","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"},{"n":"6-1106","t":"Time","s":"Time is computed under Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-2221 (a period ending on a weekend or court holiday runs to the next open day), and e-filing time under § 2-206. The court may extend a rule-based time for good cause if asked before it expires, or after it expires for excusable neglect, but may not extend a time fixed by statute except as the statute allows. When service is by mail, 3 days are added after the period would otherwise expire (the rule’s comment shows this is applied after any weekend or holiday rollover).","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"},{"n":"6-1107","t":"Pleadings allowed; form of motions","s":"Allowed pleadings: complaint, answer, counterclaim or cross-claim (which must be stated in the answer, not as a separate pleading), answers to a counterclaim, cross-claim, or third-party complaint, a third-party complaint, and a reply only if ordered. The response to a counterclaim is called an “answer,” not a reply. Written motions need a caption with court, title, file number, and motion title, must use the § 2-103(A) format, and must meet any statutory requirements, including the notice requirements of Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-910.","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"},{"n":"6-1108","t":"General rules of pleading","s":"A claim requires a caption, a short and plain statement showing entitlement to relief, and a demand for relief. Unlike federal practice, the amount of general damages must not be stated; each category of special damages and their total must be stated; and if prejudgment interest is sought, the start date must be stated. A general denial is allowed if the party has a good-faith basis to deny at least one material allegation (preserving the code-pleading standard, looser than FRCP 8(b)(3)). Allegations other than the amount of damages are admitted if not denied. Affirmative defenses must be pleaded; compared with FRCP 8(c), the list adds absolute or qualified immunity, drops injury by fellow servant, and says “claim or issue preclusion” instead of res judicata. Pleadings are construed to do substantial justice (Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-801.01).","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"},{"n":"6-1109","t":"Pleading special matters","s":"Capacity, authority, and legal existence need not be alleged and are challenged by specific denial. Fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity; state of mind may be alleged generally. Undue influence was removed from the particularity list in 2025, though its supporting facts must still be pleaded. Conditions precedent may be alleged generally but must be denied with particularity. Official documents, judgments, and time and place are treated as in FRCP 9; special damages are now covered by § 6-1108.","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"},{"n":"6-1110","t":"Form of pleadings","s":"Every pleading needs a caption with the court, title, file number, and § 6-1107(a) designation; the complaint must name all parties. Claims and defenses go in numbered paragraphs, with separate counts where that promotes clarity. Statements may be adopted by reference, and written-instrument exhibits are part of the pleading. Mirrors FRCP 10.","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"},{"n":"6-1111","t":"Signing of pleadings; assistance to unrepresented parties","s":"Every pleading, motion, and document must be signed by an attorney of record or by a self-represented party (e-signatures under § 2-201(M)), and must state the signer’s address, email, phone, and the attorney’s bar number. An unsigned paper filing is struck unless promptly corrected. Verification is not required unless a statute requires it. Unlike FRCP 11, there are no sanctions provisions; frivolous filings are handled by fee-shifting under Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-824. Ghostwriting and limited appearances are governed by Neb. Ct. R. of Prof. Cond. § 3-501.2(c)-(e).","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"},{"n":"6-1112","t":"Defenses and objections; motion for judgment on the pleadings","s":"Answer within 30 days after service of summons and complaint or completion of service by publication (not 21 as in FRCP 12). Answers to counterclaims and cross-claims are due in 30 days. A court-ordered reply is due in 21 days. After denial of a motion, or service of a more definite statement, the answer is due in 21 days. Defenses that may be raised by motion: subject-matter and personal jurisdiction, pendency of another action between the same parties on the same subject (in place of FRCP 12(b)(3) venue, which in Nebraska is raised by a motion to transfer under § 25-403.01), process, service, failure to state a claim, and failure to join a party under § 25-323. Personal jurisdiction, process, and service defenses are waived if omitted from a motion or the answer. A party may not file a second pre-answer motion raising an omitted defense. A more definite statement must be provided within 14 days of the order. A motion to strike is due before responding (or within 30 days if no response is allowed). Matters outside the pleadings convert the motion to summary judgment under Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 25-1330 to 25-1336. On another action pending, the court may dismiss or stay.","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"},{"n":"6-1113","t":"Counterclaim and crossclaim","s":"Counterclaims are permissive. There is no compulsory-counterclaim bar like FRCP 13(a); the only rule-based consequence of not pleading a same-transaction counterclaim is losing costs in a later action on it (though claim or issue preclusion may still apply, per the comment). A counterclaim may exceed or differ from the opposing claim. The rules do not expand counterclaims against the State or political subdivisions. Counterclaims that mature or are acquired later may be added by supplemental pleading. Cross-claims must arise from the same transaction or occurrence or relate to property at issue. Adding parties is governed by Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 25-323 and 25-705(4).","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"},{"n":"6-1114","t":"Third-party practice [Reserved]","s":"No text. Third-party practice is governed by Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-331.","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"},{"n":"6-1115","t":"Amended and supplemental pleadings","s":"One amendment as of course is allowed until the earlier of 30 days after serving the pleading or, if a response is required, 14 days after service of the first responsive pleading or first § 6-1112(b), (e), or (f) motion (shorter than FRCP 15’s 21 days). Otherwise, amendment requires written consent or leave, which should be freely given. The response to an amended pleading is due within the time remaining or 14 days after service, whichever is later. Amendments during trial and issues tried by consent follow FRCP 15(b); the 2025 text confines these to trial, not summary judgment. Relation back is governed by statute (Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-201.02), not the rule. The court may permit supplemental pleadings on motion and reasonable notice.","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"},{"n":"6-1116","t":"Pretrial conferences; scheduling; management [Reserved]","s":"No text. Pretrial conferences are governed by § 6-1421 (county court) and § 6-1522 (district court). The district court rule allows, but does not require, a pretrial conference after the issues are joined; its order controls unless modified at trial to prevent manifest injustice. There is no FRCP 16(b) mandatory scheduling order.","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"},{"n":"6-301","t":"Promulgating order","s":"The discovery rules were adopted under Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-1273.01, effective January 1, 1983. They apply as written in the district courts, and in other Nebraska courts to the extent consistent with statutes. They are cited by the original rule numbers (Rule 26 = § 6-326).","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"},{"n":"6-302 to 6-325","t":"[Reserved]","s":"No text. The numbering starts at § 6-326 so that the last two digits match the corresponding Federal Rule numbers.","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"},{"n":"6-326","t":"General provisions governing discovery","s":"Discovery methods include required (expert) disclosures. Nebraska has no general FRCP 26(a)(1) initial disclosures or 26(f) conference. Scope keeps the pre-2015 federal standard: any nonprivileged matter relevant to the subject matter, discoverable if reasonably calculated to lead to admissible evidence. Proportionality is not part of the scope test; it is a ground for a protective order. Insurance agreements are discoverable, and an interrogatory may ask whether coverage is disputed. Work product, privilege logs, and a non-waiver rule for inadvertent production (covering all privileges) are included. Expert disclosures follow FRCP 26(a)(2), with signed reports from retained experts; a treating physician need not write a report merely to address causation or prognosis. Absent an order or stipulation, expert disclosures are due 180 days after the first responsive pleading, and rebuttal disclosures 45 days after the other side’s. Report-required experts may be deposed only after the report. Protective-order motions require a good-faith conference certification and must be filed in the court where the action is pending. Supplementation must be timely.","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"},{"n":"6-327","t":"Depositions to perpetuate testimony","s":"Before suit, file a verified petition in the district court of the county where an expected adverse party resides, and serve notice at least 21 days before the hearing. The court appoints an attorney for adverse parties served by publication, and a guardian ad litem for minors. The deposition is usable in a later Nebraska action on the same subject. Pending appeal, move in the appellate court, which may decide or remand the motion. Tracks FRCP 27.","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"},{"n":"6-328","t":"Persons before whom depositions may be taken","s":"In Nebraska, before an officer authorized to administer oaths under Nebraska law. Elsewhere in the U.S., before an officer authorized by federal or local law, or a court-appointed person. Abroad, under a treaty, a letter of request, on notice before an authorized person, or by commission. Relatives, employees, and attorneys of a party or counsel, and anyone financially interested, are disqualified. Mirrors FRCP 28.","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"},{"n":"6-329","t":"Stipulations about discovery procedure","s":"Parties may stipulate in writing or on the record to deposition logistics and other discovery procedures. A stipulation extending discovery time needs court approval if it would interfere with the discovery deadline, a motion hearing, or trial. Mirrors FRCP 29.","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"},{"n":"6-330","t":"Depositions by oral examination","s":"Leave is needed if the plaintiff deposes within 30 days after service of summons (unless stipulated, the defendant has noticed a deposition, or the deponent is about to leave Nebraska), for a second deposition of the same witness, or for a prisoner. There is no FRCP 30 ten-deposition or one-day/7-hour limit. Depositions may be in person, by video, or by phone, and any party may give notice that it will attend by a different method. The notice must state the method, place or link or call-in, interpreter needs (or a prescribed advisory statement), and the recording method. An unknown party may be notified by publication at least 10 days before. When a needed interpreter is used, the noticing party must arrange and pay for a certified interpreter (or a registered one if none is available). Organizational (30(b)(6)) designations require a good-faith conference. Objections must be concise and nonsuggestive, and instructions not to answer are limited as in FRCP 30. If review is requested before the deposition ends, the deponent has 30 days after notice of availability to review and sign changes. Accuracy objections are waived without a review request.","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"},{"n":"6-330(A)","t":"Interstate deposition and discovery","s":"Nebraska’s version of the Uniform Interstate Depositions and Discovery Act. Submit a request in the form in the rule’s appendix, with the foreign subpoena and a counsel list, to the district court clerk of the county where discovery will occur, and pay $75 per subpoena (and per reissue). The clerk issues a Nebraska subpoena. Requesting it is not an appearance or the unauthorized practice of law, but a requesting lawyer not admitted in Nebraska certifies home-state admission and no discipline. Nebraska discovery law applies. Motions to quash, enforce, or for protection are filed as a civil action in that county’s district court, and only by a Nebraska-admitted attorney or a self-represented individual.","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"},{"n":"6-331","t":"Depositions by written questions","s":"Available without leave except in the same situations as oral depositions (a plaintiff within 30 days after service, a repeat deposition, or a prisoner). Serve the questions with a notice naming the deponent and officer (and any interpreter). Cross-questions are due within 14 days, redirect within 7 days, and recross within 7 days. The officer takes and certifies the deposition, and the noticing party notifies all parties when it is complete.","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"},{"n":"6-332","t":"Using depositions in court proceedings","s":"A deposition may be used against a party who was present, represented, or had notice, to the extent the evidence rules would admit it. An adverse party’s deposition (or that of its officer or designee) may be used for any purpose. Any witness’s deposition may be used if the witness is dead, more than 100 miles away or out of state or beyond subpoena, unable to attend, or not procurable by subpoena, or in exceptional circumstances. Unlike FRCP 32, it may also be used on a motion made before the deposition is taken. Depositions from earlier actions on the same subject between the same parties may be used. In a jury trial, testimony offered other than for impeachment must be presented by audio or video on request, if available, with reasonable notice. Waiver of objections follows FRCP 32(d); an objection to a written question is waived unless served within the time for responsive questions (7 days for recross).","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"},{"n":"6-333","t":"Interrogatories to parties","s":"Limit: 50 interrogatories per party served on any other party, including discrete subparts (FRCP 33 allows 25). More may be allowed for good cause. Contention interrogatories are allowed. They may be served on the plaintiff after commencement and on others with or after the summons, and an editable electronic copy must be provided on demand. Answers and objections are due in 30 days, except a defendant gets the longer of 45 days after service of summons or 30 days after service. Each interrogatory must be reproduced, then answered under oath. Objections must be specific or they are waived. The business-records option is available.","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"},{"n":"6-334","t":"Producing documents, ESI, and things; entry onto land","s":"Requests to parties within the § 6-326(b) scope. Same service timing and editable-copy rule as interrogatories. Responses are due in 30 days, or for a defendant the longer of 45 days after the summons or 30 days after service. Each request must be reproduced. Objections must be specific, state whether anything is being withheld, and specify any partial objection. ESI form rules track FRCP 34(b)(2)(D)-(E). Production must be completed by the inspection date or another reasonable time stated in the response.","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"},{"n":"6-334(A)","t":"Subpoenas to nonparties for documents, ESI, things, or entry","s":"The Nebraska substitute for the document-production part of FRCP 45. Serve written notice on all parties at least 14 days before the subpoena is issued; a plaintiff needs leave or a stipulation to serve the notice within 30 days after service of summons unless a defendant has already served one. Any party may object before issuance (privilege, scope, or unreasonable intrusiveness), and the subpoena may not command the objected-to items until the objection is withdrawn or overruled. The clerk, or an attorney authorized to practice in the court, issues it. It must include the text of the rule and be served at least 14 days before compliance, with copies to all parties. The nonparty may serve a written objection within 14 days (nonprivilege grounds are waived if not stated), or move to quash before compliance or within 14 days, whichever is earlier. The issuer must avoid undue burden, and sanctions may include lost earnings and fees. A deposition may be combined with the subpoena under Neb. Rev. Stat. § 25-1224.","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"},{"n":"6-335","t":"Physical and mental examinations","s":"On motion for good cause and notice, the court may order a party (or a person in its custody or control) whose condition is in controversy to be examined by a suitably licensed or certified examiner. The order specifies the time, place, manner, conditions, scope, and examiner. Report exchange follows FRCP 35(b); an examiner’s testimony may be excluded if the report is not delivered.","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"},{"n":"6-336","t":"Requests for admission","s":"Requests may cover facts, application of law to fact, opinions, and genuineness of documents; they may be served on the plaintiff after commencement and on others with or after the summons, with an editable copy on demand. A matter is admitted unless answered or objected to within 30 days, or for a defendant the longer of 45 days after the summons or 30 days after service. Lack of knowledge requires a reasonable-inquiry statement. Unlike interrogatories and production requests, objections need only state their grounds, not specificity. Admissions are conclusive unless withdrawn on the FRCP 36(b) standard.","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"},{"n":"6-337","t":"Failure to make disclosures or cooperate; sanctions","s":"Motions to compel require a good-faith conference certification and go to the court where the action is pending. Expenses including fees are mandatory against the losing side unless substantially justified or unjust. Sanctions for violating an order follow FRCP 37(b), and expenses are mandatory. On failure to admit, the requesting party may move within 30 days after proving the matter for its costs of proof, including fees. Sanctions for failing to attend one’s own deposition, answer interrogatories, respond to a production request, or make expert disclosures follow FRCP 37(d). ESI loss follows FRCP 37(e): curative measures on prejudice, and adverse inference, dismissal, or default only on intent to deprive.","u":"https://supremecourt.nebraska.gov/supreme-court-rules/chapter-6-trial-courts"}]});
